  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the solution(s) to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+2=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution.",
